From: omarchy-dragon-sl7 <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 20:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] SL7 local: leds: qcom-flash: IR emitter safety lockout

SL7 local: leds: qcom-flash: IR emitter safety lockout

The Surface Laptop 7 IR illuminator is a PM8550 flash LED driven by
Windows at 700 mA, on a channel nobody has identified yet. The stock
driver is not safe to point at an unknown eye-facing emitter: torch mode
disables the hardware safety timer, probe and remove() leave the
channel state alone, and the v4l2-flash sub-device exposes torch and
external strobe to user space. This patch adds, for LED nodes marked
color = <LED_COLOR_ID_IR> only (every other LED behaves as before):

 - Torch lockout. brightness_set_blocking() refuses any non-zero value
   with -EPERM and only switches the channel off for zero. set_flash_current()
   refuses TORCH_MODE with a non-zero current as a second check, the
   external strobe op refuses, and no v4l2-flash sub-device is created.
   The LED core queues brightness writes, so a sysfs write of 255 still
   returns success; the refusal is in the log ("torch refused") and the
   channel stays off.
 - Hard clamps in code, below whatever the device tree says: flash
   current at most 700 mA in total, flash timeout at most 100 ms, torch
   clamp at most 5 mA per channel. The safety timer is never disabled for
   an IR LED (the timeout is clamped to at least one 10 ms step). The
   default flash current is the lowest step.
 - Safety timer encoding. The CHAN_TIMER field (bit 7 enable, bits 6:0
   count) very likely counts (n + 1) x 10 ms, not the n x 10 ms the stock
   driver programs: its own 1280 ms ceiling does not fit n x 10 ms in 7
   bits, and the reset value 0x13 is a round 200 ms only as (19 + 1) x 10.
   For an IR LED the driver therefore writes n = timeout / 10 - 1 (at
   least 0, which is 10 ms), so the hardware pulse equals the requested one
   and the 100 ms clamp is a real 100 ms (not about 110 ms). If the field
   is in fact n x 10 ms, the pulse is one step shorter than requested,
   never longer. Not yet measured on the PMIC. Other LEDs keep the stock
   encoding.
 - All channels and the module are switched off in remove() and in a new
   .shutdown(), whatever the software state. A register snapshot is
   logged again at remove, after the all-off.
 - A read-only register snapshot at probe, one dev_info line per register
   (STATUS, CHAN_TIMER, ITARGET, MODULE_EN, IRESOLUTION, CHAN_STROBE,
   CHAN_EN and the thermal and torch clamp registers), each line named by
   its register (4ch: CHAN_TIMER 0x3e-0x41, ITARGET 0x42-0x45, CHAN_STROBE
   0x4a-0x4d), taken before the driver's first write. It shows what the
   firmware or Windows left in the PMIC (the IR channel, if Windows
   configured it, has ITARGET 0x37 for 700 mA; 0x3e-0x41 are the safety
   timers, which read 0x93 at reset).
 - A bind gate: the probe refuses any node set containing an IR LED unless
   the module parameter ir_test=1 (read-only at run time), before any
   register is touched. The parameter is set only by the modprobe
   gate shipped in omarchy-surface-sl7 (it requires sl7.ir_test=1 on the
   kernel command line), so a normal boot, a plain modprobe --ignore-install
   or an insmod can not bind the driver to the IR nodes.

While there: the v4l2-flash release loops started at index leds_count,
which is one past the last LED in remove() (an out of bounds read, and a
bogus pointer for any LED without a v4l2-flash device, such as the IR
ones). Replace both by a loop over the LEDs registered so far that skips
NULL entries. Drop the mutex_destroy() in remove(): the devm-managed LED
class devices are unregistered after remove() returns and switch
themselves off through callbacks that take that lock.

Hardware strobe (the qcom,ir-hw-strobe property of the plan) is not
added here: nothing consumes it before Stage C, which adds it.

Rationale: SL7 local safety layer for the IR emitter bring-up (see
research/omarchy-dragon-sl7/ir/EMITTER-PLAN.md, stage A). Not for
upstream as it stands.

Origin: ours
Upstream-Status: Not submitted
---
--- a/drivers/leds/flash/leds-qcom-flash.c
+++ b/drivers/leds/flash/leds-qcom-flash.c
@@ -5,12 +5,15 @@
 
 #include <linux/bitfield.h>
 #include <linux/bits.h>
+#include <linux/dev_printk.h>
 #include <linux/leds.h>
 #include <linux/led-class-flash.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/property.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/leds/common.h>
 #include <media/v4l2-flash-led-class.h>
 
 /* registers definitions */
@@ -76,6 +79,45 @@
 
 #define UA_PER_MA			1000
 
+/*
+ * SL7 local: IR illuminator safety (omarchy-dragon-sl7, not for upstream).
+ *
+ * An LED node with color = <LED_COLOR_ID_IR> drives the Surface Laptop 7 IR
+ * emitter. The device tree already carries per-LED limits; these are a second,
+ * independent layer in code that no device tree edit can raise. For an IR LED:
+ *  - torch mode is refused (the PMIC safety timer is off in torch mode),
+ *  - the flash current is clamped to SL7_IR_FLASH_MAX_UA in total, the torch
+ *    clamp register to TORCH_IRES_UA per channel,
+ *  - the safety timer is always on and at most SL7_IR_TIMEOUT_MAX_MS of
+ *    hardware time (see SL7_IR_TIMER_N below for the encoding),
+ *  - no v4l2-flash sub-device is created (no torch, no external strobe),
+ *  - the driver binds only when the module parameter ir_test is set.
+ */
+#define SL7_IR_FLASH_MAX_UA		700000
+#define SL7_IR_TIMEOUT_MAX_MS		100
+#define SL7_IR_TIMEOUT_MIN_MS		FLASH_TIMER_STEP_MS
+
+/*
+ * Safety timer encoding. Upstream writes n = timeout_ms / 10, but the field
+ * very likely counts (n + 1) x 10 ms: the 7-bit field is documented as
+ * 10 ms - 1280 ms (FLASH_TIMEOUT_MAX_US = 1280000 does not fit n x 10 ms, which
+ * tops out at 1270 ms), and the reset value 0x13 gives a round 200 ms only as
+ * (19 + 1) x 10. Not yet measured on the PMIC. For an IR LED the code therefore
+ * programs n = timeout_ms / 10 - 1 (at least 0, which is 10 ms), so the
+ * hardware pulse equals the requested one and the 100 ms ceiling is a real
+ * 100 ms. If the field is in fact n x 10 ms, n is one step low: the pulse is
+ * shorter than requested (never longer), so every limit holds either way.
+ * Effective charge at the 12.5 mA discovery limit: 12.5 mA x 10 ms = 0.125 mC
+ * per pulse; at the 100 ms ceiling and 700 mA, 70 mC. Other LEDs keep the
+ * upstream encoding (an upstream matter, if the off-by-one is real).
+ */
+#define SL7_IR_TIMER_N(ms)		((ms) / FLASH_TIMER_STEP_MS - 1)
+
+static bool ir_test;
+module_param(ir_test, bool, 0400);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(ir_test,
+		 "SL7 local: allow binding to IR emitter LED nodes (set only by the sl7.ir_test=1 modprobe gate, default off)");
+
 /* thermal threshold constants */
 #define OTST_3CH_MIN_VAL		3
 #define OTST1_4CH_MIN_VAL		0
@@ -167,6 +209,8 @@
 
 struct qcom_flash_data {
 	struct v4l2_flash	**v4l2_flash;
+	struct regmap		*regmap;	/* SL7 local: for the remove snapshot */
+	u32			reg_base;
 	struct regmap_field     *r_fields[REG_MAX_COUNT];
 	struct mutex		lock;
 	enum hw_type		hw_type;
@@ -190,6 +234,7 @@
 	u8				*chan_id;
 	u8				chan_count;
 	bool				enabled;
+	bool				ir;	/* SL7 local: IR emitter, see SL7_IR_* */
 };
 
 static int set_flash_module_en(struct qcom_flash_led *led, bool en)
@@ -344,6 +389,14 @@
 	u8 shift, ires_mask = 0, ires_val = 0, chan_id;
 	int i, rc;
 
+	if (led->ir) {
+		/* SL7 local: no torch for the IR emitter, hard flash ceiling */
+		if (mode == TORCH_MODE && current_ma)
+			return -EPERM;
+		current_ma = min_t(u32, current_ma,
+				   SL7_IR_FLASH_MAX_UA / UA_PER_MA);
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * Split the current across the channels and set the
 	 * IRESOLUTION and ITARGET registers accordingly.
@@ -393,11 +446,18 @@
 
 	/* set SAFETY_TIMER for all the channels connected to the same LED */
 	timeout_ms = min_t(u32, timeout_ms, led->max_timeout_ms);
+	if (led->ir) {
+		/* SL7 local: the timer is never disabled, never above 100 ms of hardware time */
+		timeout_ms = clamp_t(u32, timeout_ms, SL7_IR_TIMEOUT_MIN_MS,
+				     SL7_IR_TIMEOUT_MAX_MS);
+	}
 
 	for (i = 0; i < led->chan_count; i++) {
 		chan_id = led->chan_id[i];
 
-		timer = timeout_ms / FLASH_TIMER_STEP_MS;
+		/* SL7 local: IR LEDs program n - 1, so hardware time == requested */
+		timer = led->ir ? SL7_IR_TIMER_N(timeout_ms) :
+				  timeout_ms / FLASH_TIMER_STEP_MS;
 		timer = clamp_t(u8, timer, 0, FLASH_TIMER_VAL_MASK);
 
 		if (timeout_ms)
@@ -448,6 +508,104 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * SL7 local: switch every channel and the module off, whatever the software
+ * state. Used on remove and on shutdown, so a reboot, kexec or module removal
+ * never leaves an emitter armed. Best effort: tries everything, returns the
+ * first error.
+ */
+static int qcom_flash_all_off(struct qcom_flash_data *flash_data)
+{
+	int rc, rc2;
+
+	rc = regmap_field_write(flash_data->r_fields[REG_CHAN_EN], 0);
+	rc2 = regmap_field_write(flash_data->r_fields[REG_MODULE_EN], 0);
+	if (!rc)
+		rc = rc2;
+
+	mutex_lock(&flash_data->lock);
+	flash_data->chan_en_bits = 0;
+	flash_data->total_ma = 0;
+	mutex_unlock(&flash_data->lock);
+
+	return rc;
+}
+
+/*
+ * SL7 local: read-only dump of the flash module registers, one dev_info line
+ * per register (never a write), each named by its register. reg_base +
+ * [first, last] per hardware type covers STATUS, CHAN_TIMER (safety timer, bit 7
+ * enable, 6:0 count), ITARGET (current), MODULE_EN, IRESOLUTION, CHAN_STROBE and
+ * CHAN_EN. On 4ch: CHAN_TIMER is 0x3e-0x41, ITARGET 0x42-0x45, CHAN_STROBE
+ * 0x4a-0x4d. Taken at probe before the first write (what the firmware, or
+ * Windows before a warm reboot, left behind) and again at remove after the
+ * channels were switched off.
+ */
+static const char *qcom_flash_reg_name(enum hw_type type, int reg)
+{
+	if (type == QCOM_MVFLASH_4CH) {
+		switch (reg) {
+		case 0x06: return "STATUS1";
+		case 0x07: return "STATUS2";
+		case 0x09: return "STATUS3";
+		case 0x3e ... 0x41: return "CHAN_TIMER";
+		case 0x42 ... 0x45: return "ITARGET";
+		case 0x46: return "MODULE_EN";
+		case 0x49: return "IRESOLUTION";
+		case 0x4a ... 0x4d: return "CHAN_STROBE";
+		case 0x4e: return "CHAN_EN";
+		case 0x78: return "THERM_THRSH2";
+		case 0x7a: return "THERM_THRSH1";
+		case 0xed: return "TORCH_CLAMP";
+		}
+		return "-";
+	}
+
+	switch (reg) {
+	case 0x08: return "STATUS1";
+	case 0x09: return "STATUS2";
+	case 0x0a: return "STATUS3";
+	case 0x40 ... 0x42: return "CHAN_TIMER";
+	case 0x43 ... 0x45: return "ITARGET";
+	case 0x46: return "MODULE_EN";
+	case 0x47: return "IRESOLUTION";
+	case 0x49 ... 0x4b: return "CHAN_STROBE";
+	case 0x4c: return "CHAN_EN";
+	case 0x56: return "THERM_THRSH1";
+	case 0x57: return "THERM_THRSH2";
+	case 0x58: return "THERM_THRSH3";
+	case 0xea: return "TORCH_CLAMP";
+	}
+	return "-";
+}
+
+static void qcom_flash_snapshot(struct device *dev, struct regmap *regmap,
+				u32 reg_base, enum hw_type type, const char *when)
+{
+	static const u8 ranges_4ch[][2] = { { 0x00, 0x09 }, { 0x3e, 0x4e }, { 0x78, 0x7a }, { 0xed, 0xed } };
+	static const u8 ranges_3ch[][2] = { { 0x00, 0x0a }, { 0x40, 0x4c }, { 0x56, 0x58 }, { 0xea, 0xec } };
+	const u8 (*ranges)[2] = type == QCOM_MVFLASH_4CH ? ranges_4ch : ranges_3ch;
+	int n = type == QCOM_MVFLASH_4CH ? ARRAY_SIZE(ranges_4ch) : ARRAY_SIZE(ranges_3ch);
+	u32 val;
+	int i, reg, rc;
+
+	dev_info(dev, "SL7 register snapshot (%s, read-only), base %#x, %s:\n",
+		 when, reg_base, type == QCOM_MVFLASH_4CH ? "4ch" : "3ch");
+	for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+		for (reg = ranges[i][0]; reg <= ranges[i][1]; reg++) {
+			rc = regmap_read(regmap, reg_base + reg, &val);
+			if (rc)
+				dev_info(dev, "SL7 snapshot %s: reg %#06x %s read failed (%d)\n",
+					 when, reg_base + reg,
+					 qcom_flash_reg_name(type, reg), rc);
+			else
+				dev_info(dev, "SL7 snapshot %s: reg %#06x %s = %#04x\n",
+					 when, reg_base + reg,
+					 qcom_flash_reg_name(type, reg), val);
+		}
+	}
+}
+
 static inline struct qcom_flash_led *flcdev_to_qcom_fled(struct led_classdev_flash *flcdev)
 {
 	return container_of(flcdev, struct qcom_flash_led, flash);
@@ -590,6 +748,33 @@
 	bool enable = !!brightness;
 	int rc;
 
+	if (led->ir) {
+		/*
+		 * SL7 local: torch has no hardware safety timer. Refuse any
+		 * non-zero brightness; zero only switches the LED off (no
+		 * register is programmed for torch). The LED core queues
+		 * brightness writes, so the sysfs write itself still returns
+		 * success: the refusal shows in the log and the channel stays
+		 * off. Reset the cached value so reads show 0.
+		 */
+		if (brightness) {
+			dev_warn_ratelimited(led_cdev->dev,
+					     "SL7: torch refused on the IR emitter LED\n");
+			led_cdev->brightness = LED_OFF;
+			return -EPERM;
+		}
+
+		rc = set_flash_strobe(led, SW_STROBE, false);
+		if (rc)
+			return rc;
+
+		rc = update_allowed_flash_current(led, &current_ma, false);
+		if (rc < 0)
+			return rc;
+
+		return set_flash_module_en(led, false);
+	}
+
 	rc = set_flash_strobe(led, SW_STROBE, false);
 	if (rc)
 		return rc;
@@ -633,6 +818,10 @@
 	struct qcom_flash_led *led = flcdev_to_qcom_fled(fled_cdev);
 	int rc;
 
+	/* SL7 local: hardware strobe is not enabled for the IR emitter yet */
+	if (led->ir)
+		return -EPERM;
+
 	rc = set_flash_module_en(led, enable);
 	if (rc)
 		return rc;
@@ -712,6 +901,24 @@
 }
 #endif
 
+/*
+ * SL7 local: release the v4l2-flash sub-devices of the LEDs registered so far.
+ * IR LEDs have none (NULL entry). Replaces the open-coded loops, which started
+ * at index leds_count (one past the last LED on remove) and so could free a
+ * pointer that was never set.
+ */
+static void qcom_flash_release_v4l2(struct qcom_flash_data *flash_data)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = (int)flash_data->leds_count - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
+		if (flash_data->v4l2_flash[i]) {
+			v4l2_flash_release(flash_data->v4l2_flash[i]);
+			flash_data->v4l2_flash[i] = NULL;
+		}
+	}
+}
+
